+# Decision: Change delivery approach
+
+## Context
+
+Editors submit commands over HTTPS and the server assigns each committed edit one event revision. Something then has to carry committed revisions to the browsers displaying the board: up to 800 simultaneous browsers per the pilot, 10-25 editors per busy market, about two edits per minute on average with opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second.
+
+Every credible delivery approach meets the project's freshness, capacity, catch-up, retention, and availability goals, so the choice does not turn on whether the board works. It turns on what the project pays for working: recurring fees, provider coupling, connection operations, and how quickly a change appears. The choice also sizes three other design nodes. The change store, delivery, and browser sync each take a different shape depending on the approach, which makes it costly to reverse once those nodes exist.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+All criteria are weighted; no option fails a gate. The standing goals (15-second freshness, 800 browsers, 20-minute catch-up, seven-day revision retention) and the tenets *a committed edit has one authoritative event revision regardless of its delivery route* and *connectivity failures must be visible* are satisfied by every option, so they do not drive selection.
+
+- **Vendor cost and provider coupling** — *major.* A realtime vendor adds a recurring fee that scales with the board's use and ties delivery behavior and credentials to a provider. Updates carry only stall IDs and operational statuses, so the tenet permitting both self-hosted and managed infrastructure leaves this open; the weight comes from the project's preference not to depend on a vendor for its core update path.
+- **Update latency** — *moderate.* The goal is 15 seconds. Sub-second updates are useful to coordinators reacting to closures and power problems, but are not required.
+- **Connection and reconnect operations burden** — *moderate.* Long-lived connections need capacity management, session handling, and reconnect and catch-up behavior that the project would own and operate.
+- **Moving parts and statelessness** — *minor.* Fewer components that hold per-browser state make operation and failure analysis easier.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** Browsers request changes since their last known revision every five seconds. Delivery is ordinary request/response and holds no per-browser state. The change store answers cursor queries ("revisions after N"), and browsers run a polling loop.
+
+**Analysis**
+- Vendor cost and provider coupling: **strong** — no vendor; runs on the same HTTPS surface as commands.
+- Update latency: **partial** — changes appear up to about five seconds after commit, plus request time; within the goal, short of sub-second.
+- Connection and reconnect operations burden: **strong** — no long-lived connections; a failed poll is simply retried.
+- Moving parts and statelessness: **strong** — stateless delivery, the simplest of the three.
+
+**Summary.** Simplest to operate, at the cost of repeated reads and a few seconds of lag.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description.** Browsers hold a WebSocket to a server the project operates, which pushes each committed revision as it is published. A browser that reconnects presents the last revision it holds and receives the revisions it missed. The change store keeps replayable revisions and publishes each committed revision reliably; delivery manages sessions and connection capacity; browser sync implements reconnect and catch-up.
+
+**Analysis**
+- Vendor cost and provider coupling: **strong** — no vendor fee and no provider dependency; infrastructure and capacity are the project's own.
+- Update latency: **strong** — committed revisions are pushed as published, typically well under a second.
+- Connection and reconnect operations burden: **weak** — the project owns connection capacity for up to 800 browsers, session lifecycle, and reconnect storms after a server restart or network event.
+- Moving parts and statelessness: **weak** — delivery holds per-browser session state, and the change store needs replayable revisions and reliable publication that polling does not.
+
+**Summary.** Fast updates with no vendor, paid for in connection operations the project runs itself.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** The server publishes each committed revision to a managed realtime provider, and browsers subscribe through the provider's client. Delivery wraps the provider; the change store reconciles what it committed against what was published; browser sync uses the provider's subscription model and its credentials.
+
+**Analysis**
+- Vendor cost and provider coupling: **weak** — recurring fees, and delivery, subscription credentials, and browser behavior follow the provider's model.
+- Update latency: **strong** — pushed delivery, typically well under a second.
+- Connection and reconnect operations burden: **strong** — connection capacity and most reconnect mechanics are the provider's.
+- Moving parts and statelessness: **partial** — no connection state in the project's servers, but a third-party dependency and a publication reconciliation path are added.
+
+**Summary.** Quickest route to low-latency updates with the least connection work, in exchange for ongoing cost and coupling.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.** It is the only option that is strong on both vendor independence (major) and update latency (moderate). Polling leaves a few seconds of lag and repeated reads, and the hosted service puts a recurring fee and provider coupling on the board's core update path. Its weakness is the connection and reconnect operations burden (moderate) and per-session state (minor), which the project accepts.
+
+Neither the tenets nor the goals decide this on their own; the outcome rests on weighing vendor independence above operational convenience. If connection operations later prove heavier than expected, the hosted service remains a viable replacement because updates contain no customer records and the tenets permit managed infrastructure; the cost of moving would be rewriting delivery and browser sync, not the change store's revision model.
+
+**Forecloses.** Stateless request/response delivery of changes, and a provider-managed subscription model.
+
+**Obligates downstream.**
+- Change store: replayable event revisions retained for seven days, and reliable publication of each committed revision to delivery.
+- Delivery: WebSocket session management and connection capacity for 800 browsers, and fan-out of published revisions.
+- Browser sync: reconnect, catch-up from the last held revision within the 20-minute recovery window, and a visible connectivity state when the socket is down, per the tenet that connectivity failures must not look like an unchanged board.
