 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes reach browsers over self-hosted WebSockets: the server pushes each committed revision to connected browsers, and a browser that reconnects catches up from the last revision it holds. Updates arrive well inside the freshness target; subsecond delivery is useful but not required.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed together around this choice. The change store keeps replayable revisions and publishes each committed revision reliably; delivery manages socket sessions and connection capacity; browser sync owns reconnect, catch-up, and visible connectivity state. The project owns its connection capacity and reconnect behavior rather than depending on a realtime vendor.
 
